      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McGrath%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "McGrath sits on the south bank of the Kuskokwim River, where the Takotna River joins.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McGrath%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Because it was the northernmost point on the Kuskokwim reachable by large boats, McGrath became a regional supply hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McGrath%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The historic Iditarod Trail helped cement McGrath’s role as a supply center for the interior.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McGrath%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1940, an airstrip opened and the FAA built a communications complex, turning McGrath into a wartime refueling stop.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
